<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 100 0 R
/T (Preamble on the Robotic Orientation of the Project)
/E (Preamble on the Robotic Orientation of the Project)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
239 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (Educational robotics is often associated with expensive kits, but the aim of this project is to demonstrate that with a simple micro:bit board, some recycled cardboard, and a bit of creativity, pupils can build a functional robot that teaches them to orient themselves in space.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
240 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (For what is a robot, if not a physical object capable of perceiving its environment \(here, the Earth's magnetic field\) and responding to it \(displaying a direction, rotating an arrow\)? No need for articulated arms or motorised wheels: our cardboard compass robot perfectly embodies this link between code and the real world. The pupil programmes instructions, and a concrete object that they have built with their own hands responds to those instructions. It is this magic of seeing the digital act upon the physical that makes robotics captivating.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (Here, pupils don't code for the sake of coding: they code to understand how a compass works, to orient themselves in their neighbourhood, to create orienteering courses. The robot is not an end in itself, but a learning companion that makes tangible abstract concepts such as cardinal points, angles, or terrestrial magnetism.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (Finally, this project demonstrates that robotics can be done on a minimal budget. A micro:bit board shared between several groups, cereal boxes, glue: these are the ingredients of a robot that is every bit as good as commercial kits, with the added advantage of being entirely customisable and understandable by pupils.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
