244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (Knowing how to orient oneself in space is a fundamental skill that humans have developed for millennia. From stars to magnetic compasses, from paper maps to GPS, orientation tools have evolved but the need remains the same: knowing where you are and which direction to go.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
245 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (In this sequence, pupils will build their own orientation tool: a cardboard robot equipped with a digital compass. This project will enable them to understand concretely how a compass works, to manipulate the concepts of cardinal points and degrees, and to develop their spatial orientation skills.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
246 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (The compass robot becomes a learning companion: its "face" \(the LED matrix of the micro:bit board\) displays an arrow pointing North, allowing pupils to orient themselves in a fun way. Building the cardboard robot adds a creative and customisable dimension to the project, whilst programming develops algorithmic thinking.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
247 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (The final objective is twofold: mastering the spatial orientation concepts in the geography curriculum, whilst discovering the possibilities offered by programming and digital sensors.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
