255 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 101 0 R
/T (Warm-up: Discovering Orientation and Compasses)
/E (Warm-up: Discovering Orientation and Compasses)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
256 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (Objective: Understand what orientation is, discover the cardinal points and how a compass works)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
257 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (Begin with an open discussion with the class: "What does it mean to orient oneself? When do you need to orient yourselves in everyday life?" Note the pupils' responses on the board. Draw out different situations: finding your way in an unfamiliar town, navigating in a forest, following a route on a map...)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
258 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (Then introduce the etymology of the word "orientation": it comes from "orient", which means the East, where the sun rises. Historically, humans found their bearings using the sun: East \(sunrise\), West \(sunset\), and from these deduced North and South.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
259 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (Present the four cardinal points: North, South, East, West. Explain their clockwise arrangement \(North, East, South, West\) and point out that opposite directions face each other \(North/South, East/West\).)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
260 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (Organise a game in the classroom: identify together where North is \(if you have a compass, use it; otherwise, base it on the position of the sun or knowledge of the neighbourhood\). Place "N", "S", "E", "W" signs at the four corresponding corners of the classroom. Then ask pupils to move about: "Go towards the North!", "Turn to face the East!". This kinaesthetic game anchors the concepts in a physical way.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
261 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 101 0 R
/E (Show a traditional compass to the class. Let pupils observe it: what do they see? A needle that moves, letters \(N, S, E, W\), graduations... Explain that the needle is magnetised and always points towards magnetic North due to the Earth's magnetic field. For the more curious, you can show a short video explaining that the Earth behaves like a gigantic magnet, with magnetic North and South poles. The compass needle, which is itself magnetised, is attracted by this magnetic field.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
